Investigate this Wazuh alert:

{
  "severity": "high",
  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "10.20.5.4", "inferred_role": "corporate_ssl_vpn_gateway_management_interface", "first_seen": "2028-05-08T14:18:42+0200", "last_seen": "2028-05-08T14:42:42+0200", "agent_name": "CORP-VPN-01"},
    {"ip": "91.219.236.87", "inferred_role": "external_attacker_source_compromised_admin_login", "first_seen": "2028-05-08T14:18:42+0200", "last_seen": "2028-05-08T14:31:42+0200"},
    {"ip": "45.95.169.142", "inferred_role": "external_c2_config_exfiltration_destination", "first_seen": "2028-05-08T14:42:42+0200", "last_seen": "2028-05-08T14:42:42+0200"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Initial Access / Valid Accounts", "technique": "T1078", "what_happened": "At 2028-05-08 14:18:42 CEST, the VPN gateway audit log (forwarded to Wazuh) recorded VPN_LOGIN_SUCCESS for admin user 'vpn.admin' from src_ip=91.219.236.87. The session_id was 's-3c41' and the auth method recorded was password+TOTP. Six seconds later at 14:18:48, the Wazuh decoder fired its baseline-deviation rule: VPN admin login from non-baseline external IP. vpn.admin's 90-day login baseline contains ONLY two source IPs (the admin's home WAN 86.213.x.x and the corporate egress 198.51.x.x); 91.219.236.87 has NEVER appeared in vpn.admin's login history. The legitimate auth-method completion (password + TOTP) plus the geographically/operationally anomalous source IP is the diagnostic signature of credential compromise WITH MFA-token compromise (likely MFA-fatigue / phishing / SIM-swap, or the TOTP seed being stolen from the admin's authenticator backup) — NOT a brute-force or exploit. The attacker logged in legitimately with stolen full credentials.", "chain_reason": "VPN audit event VPN_LOGIN_SUCCESS with username='vpn.admin' + src_ip=91.219.236.87 outside the 90-day baseline IP set for the same user. Custom Wazuh rule on baseline-deviation. Diagnostic of stolen-credential-with-MFA-token compromise rather than exploit.", "first_seen": "2028-05-08T14:18:42+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Discovery / System Information Discovery", "technique": "T1082", "what_happened": "At 14:22:14 CEST, ~3 min 32 s after login, the VPN gateway audit log recorded CONFIG_VIEW: admin='vpn.admin' executed 'view full running-config' (read-only browsing of the running configuration). For a legitimate admin operation this would be the first step of a troubleshoot or audit; for an attacker it is the orientation step before exfil — verifying which secrets are stored where in the config before downloading it. The 3-minute gap after login is consistent with attacker tooling delay (manual session, deciding what to do next) rather than scripted exploit (which would fire actions in seconds).", "chain_reason": "VPN audit event CONFIG_VIEW with admin='vpn.admin' + action='view full running-config'. Read-only access, no modification. Diagnostic of reconnaissance step preceding extraction.", "first_seen": "2028-05-08T14:22:14+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Collection / Credentials in Files", "technique": "T1552.001 + T1602.002", "what_happened": "At 14:24:42 CEST, the VPN gateway audit log recorded CONFIG_EXPORT: admin='vpn.admin' executed 'download config.tar.gz' with size=428 KB. The exported tarball is the FULL appliance configuration containing the entire VPN authentication backbone: LDAP bind credentials (the service-account password used to authenticate users against AD), RADIUS shared secret (used by ALL RADIUS clients across the enterprise to integrate with the VPN's MFA service), IPSec PSKs for ALL site-to-site tunnels (every branch office and partner connection uses one of these PSKs to establish their IPSec tunnel), SSL/TLS server certificates including private keys (the VPN's own server cert that clients trust), and any local-admin password hashes. ALL of these are now considered EXFILTRATED to 91.219.236.87 (and onward to 45.95.169.142 per step 5). Unlike a zero-day exploit (which leaves a clear forensic boundary), this attacker came in with VALID admin credentials = every action looks legitimate to the appliance and the only forensic boundary is the unfamiliar source IP and the abnormally short session pattern.", "chain_reason": "VPN audit event CONFIG_EXPORT with admin='vpn.admin' + action='download config.tar.gz' + size=428 KB ~2 min 28 s after the read-only CONFIG_VIEW. The view-then-export sequence is the signature pattern: attacker confirmed the secrets are present, then exfiltrated.", "first_seen": "2028-05-08T14:24:42+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Discovery / Account Discovery", "technique": "T1087", "what_happened": "At 14:25:18 CEST (36 seconds after the config export), the VPN gateway audit log recorded USER_LIST_EXPORT: admin='vpn.admin' executed 'export user list with last-login'. The exported list contains EVERY VPN user account (employees, contractors, third-party vendors) plus their last-login timestamps and source IPs. This is reconnaissance for FUTURE targeted attacks — the attacker now knows: (a) which user accounts exist, (b) which ones are active (recent last-login), (c) where they typically connect from. This list will be used to craft targeted phishing campaigns, password-spray candidate selection, or to identify dormant accounts that could be hijacked with low detection risk. The dormant-account angle is operationally significant: an account with last-login >180 days ago is unlikely to be missed by its owner if hijacked, and may have weak/old passwords AND no MFA configured.", "chain_reason": "VPN audit event USER_LIST_EXPORT immediately after CONFIG_EXPORT. The two-export-then-leave pattern is the signature of a credentials+intel theft mission rather than an opportunistic misuse.", "first_seen": "2028-05-08T14:25:18+0200"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Command and Control / Exfiltration", "technique": "T1041", "what_happened": "At 14:31:42 CEST, the VPN gateway audit log recorded SESSION_ENDED for 'vpn.admin' session 's-3c41'. Total session duration: 12 min 60 s ~ 13 min — abnormally short for an admin session (vpn.admin's 90-day baseline session duration: mean 47 min, median 38 min, p95 92 min, observed minimum 8 min for emergency-fix sessions). Then at 14:42:42 CEST (11 minutes AFTER the admin session ended), Suricata flagged OUTBOUND HTTPS from CORP-VPN-01's management interface (data.src_ip=10.20.5.4) to data.dest_ip=45.95.169.142, data.dest_port=443. The destination IP is EXTERNAL (non-RFC1918) and the management interface should NEVER initiate outbound traffic to arbitrary internet IPs — its operational outbound profile is vendor update HTTPS to a known FQDN allowlist, NTP, DNS, and that is essentially it. The destination 45.95.169.142 is the attacker's C2 receiving the exfiltrated config.tar.gz + user list. The 11-minute gap between session-end and outbound exfil is consistent with the appliance having an outbound proxy that batches/queues data before transmission, OR with an attacker-installed agent waiting for a quiet moment.", "chain_reason": "Suricata network flow: data.src_ip=10.20.5.4 (CORP-VPN-01 mgmt) + data.dest_ip=45.95.169.142 + data.dest_port=443. Combined with the preceding CONFIG_EXPORT + USER_LIST_EXPORT, the diagnostic chain is exfil of the downloaded artifacts to attacker-controlled infrastructure.", "first_seen": "2028-05-08T14:42:42+0200"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2028-05-08T14:18:42+0200", "end": "2028-05-08T14:42:42+0200"},
  "alert_count": 7,
  "unique_steps": 5
}
